module match_applied_sum.neg_2

// An arm naming a variant of ANOTHER sum (`Ok` of `Result`) in a `match` on an
// `Option` -- refused by name, exactly as on a plain sum.
//
// Why the cell exists: the arms of a match on an applied sum are judged
// through the DECLARATION of its head, so `Some` and `None` are found and `Ok`
// is not. Before this wave the question never arose (the whole match was
// refused at its head); now it is the one thing that keeps a foreign tag out
// of the emitted test. The oracle refuses the same program
// (E_MATCH_FOREIGN_VARIANT).

// NOVAC_EXPECT names a variant of a different sum

fn first(o Option[int]) -> int => match o {
    Some(v) => v
    Ok(_) => 0
    None => 1
}

fn main() {
    println(first(Some(1)))
}
